#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SessionStart hook for the no-cape plugin.
#
# LEAN MODE (frontier-class models — the original no-cape stance):
# Injects a neutral roster of the no-cape skills and WHEN each applies, so the
# agent is aware they exist without having to scan the skill list first. This
# is deliberately NOT a "you MUST invoke" directive — that would make no-cape
# the very coercion framework it was built to replace. The list points; the
# model judges; the skill itself holds the discipline.
#
# The exceptions are the checkpoints below. The two front-of-project
# disciplines fire only ONCE per project — miss the moment and it's gone,
# unlike the loop skills that recur. Pure opt-in loses exactly there: the model
# slips past the decision without ever weighing it. Three more ride on pointers
# from user-prompt-submit (root-cause-debugging, receiving-feedback,
# blast-radius): a cause stated without evidence leaves the session and is
# carried forward as fact; a wrong rebuttal defends the error for another
# round; a blast-radius miss surfaces in production for someone else. Every
# checkpoint forces the ACTION (pause and state your read), never the
# CONCLUSION (adopting or skipping stay equally valid). They make skipping a
# visible, accountable choice instead of silent momentum — the minimum
# coercion that fixes the miss, scoped to the skills that can't recover from
# it. Recurring disciplines never qualify: the next diff catches those.
#
# Lean mode also carries TWO kernels inline (root-cause-debugging's evidence
# rule, verify-before-done's fresh-run rule). That is knowledge, not coercion:
# the same floor cape mode gives every skill, applied in lean to the two
# disciplines whose miss leaves the session as a stated fact.
# verify-before-done gets a kernel rather than a checkpoint because its moment
# is the end of the model's own turn, which no prompt scanner can see.
#
# CAPE MODE (everything below frontier class):
# The same knowledge, delivered coercively. Weaker tiers fail differently: they
# don't lack the discipline, they miss the MOMENT to load it, and they follow
# short in-context rules far better than judgment calls. So cape mode injects a
# directive roster plus the distilled kernel of every skill (a portable
# mini-CLAUDE.md), turns the UserPromptSubmit keyword pointers into directives,
# and arms the verification gate at Stop. Mode resolution and the
# per-session cache live in common.sh; this hook is the only one that sees the
# model id, so it resolves once and caches for the others.

set -euo pipefail

# ${0%[/\\]*} = dirname without a spawn; handles both path separators because
# run-hook.cmd hands us a Windows-style $0.
. "${0%[/\\]*}/common.sh"

# Hook input arrives on stdin as JSON (Claude Code). Other platforms may pipe
# nothing — degrade to lean, which matches pre-2.0 behavior.
INPUT=""
read_stdin INPUT
json_str_v SESSION_ID "$INPUT" "session_id"
json_str_v MODEL "$INPUT" "model"
json_str_v CWD_RAW "$INPUT" "cwd"
json_unescape_v CWD "$CWD_RAW"

resolve_mode_v MODE "$CWD" "$MODEL"
cache_mode "$SESSION_ID" "$MODE" "$MODEL"

# The shared roster: skill name → the situation that calls for it.
read -r -d '' ROSTER <<'EOF' || true
- exploring-approaches - a vague new idea or goal arrives; settle the project's *shape* (runs first, before clarifying-requirements)
- clarifying-requirements - the shape is chosen; pin down underspecified details within it
- writing-solid-plans - planning a multi-step feature or refactor
- durable-plans - a plan must outlive the session: multi-session work, resuming from an on-disk plan, or landing changes one describes
- surgical-changes - editing existing code you didn't just write
- fail-loud - writing error handling, fallbacks, or retries
- safe-refactoring - restructuring working code
- blast-radius - changing a condition, guard, filter, or allow/deny rule that existing code already reaches
- writing-meaningful-tests - writing or modifying tests
- measure-before-optimizing - performance work
- schema-migration-safety - changing a database schema
- root-cause-debugging - a bug, test failure, or regression
- verify-before-done - about to claim done/fixed/passing
- receiving-feedback - acting on review comments or corrections
- escalate-dont-thrash - repeated attempts at the same problem failing without new evidence; stop and hand off
EOF

if [ "$MODE" = "cape" ]; then
    # Tone is load-bearing here too — in the opposite direction. Weak tiers
    # under-trigger on "use judgment", so cape mode says "load it BEFORE the
    # work" and carries each skill's kernel inline as always-on rules. The
    # kernels mirror the README table; keep both in sync.
    read -r -d '' NOCAPE_CONTEXT <<EOF || true
<no-cape mode="cape">
Cape mode is active: engineering discipline in this session is enforced, not optional. The full disciplines live in the no-cape skills (load via the Skill tool). When a situation below arises, load the matching skill BEFORE doing the work. Reminders injected during the session are directives, not suggestions.

Situation → skill:
${ROSTER}

Kernels, always in effect (the skill holds the full discipline; these are the non-negotiable cores):
1. No fix without a stated root cause backed by evidence from this incident. Reproduce the bug before fixing; one hypothesis at a time; never stack fixes. Comments, commit messages, and memory are claims, not evidence — verify a load-bearing premise against docs, types, or a quick experiment before building on it.
2. The diff is the product: every changed line traces back to the request. No drive-by cleanups — report them instead of applying them.
3. Never mix restructuring with behavior change. Green before, between, after.
4. A swallowed error is a bug with a delay timer. No silent fallbacks, no empty catch blocks.
5. Test behavior, not mocks. A test you have never seen fail proves nothing.
6. No optimization without a baseline measurement of the real workload.
7. Schema changes: old code runs against the new schema during every deploy. Expand–contract, tested rollbacks.
8. "Done", "fixed", "passing" require a fresh verification command run THIS turn — lint ≠ build ≠ tests. Report failures honestly, never optimism.
9. A plan that must outlive the session lives on disk, one file per change. When resuming, read the plan then verify it against the code — the code wins. Update the plan in the same commit that deviates from it; retire it when the change lands.
10. Two failed attempts at the same problem without new evidence → stop. Write a handoff summary (verified facts, eliminated hypotheses, what each failed attempt changed) and recommend escalation to a stronger model or a human. Never a third variation.
11. Before changing who passes a condition, enumerate every path that reaches it and check who hits it in production. Implicit pass-through is a dependency — make it explicit, don't break it.

Required checkpoint: before starting a new piece of work — and before writing any code for it — state your read on exploring-approaches (is the project's shape settled?) and clarifying-requirements (are the details specified enough to design?). Adopting one, or judging both unnecessary, are equally valid — but say which, out loud.
</no-cape>
EOF
else
    # Lean mode. Tone is load-bearing: "NOT mandatory" / "use judgment" keeps
    # the decision with the model.
    read -r -d '' NOCAPE_CONTEXT <<EOF || true
<no-cape>
This session has the no-cape skills available - lean engineering-discipline skills. They are NOT mandatory; load one via the Skill tool when its situation actually arises. Each line is when it applies:

${ROSTER}

Use judgment: skip the ones that don't apply. The skill holds the actual discipline; this list only points you to it. When a later prompt's wording matches one of these situations, a one-line <no-cape-pointer> names the skill at that moment — it is a pointer, not a directive; it exists because the moment to load a discipline is easy to miss mid-flow, and the decision is still yours.

Two kernels stay in effect whether or not you ever load the skill:
- root-cause-debugging: no stated cause and no fix without evidence from this incident. Comments, commit messages, and memory are claims, not evidence — a conclusion that rests on one says so, and a load-bearing premise about how a library behaves at runtime gets checked against docs, types, or a one-minute experiment before anything is built on it.
- verify-before-done: "done", "fixed", "passing" require a verification command run fresh in this turn with its output read; a failing state is reported as it is, never as optimism.

Required checkpoints (the rest of the roster stays judgment-only). Each forces an action, never a conclusion: you say your read out loud, and either answer is fine.
1. Front of project: before starting a new piece of work — and before writing any code for it — pause once and state your read on exploring-approaches (is the project's shape settled?) and clarifying-requirements (are the details specified enough to design?). Adopting one, or judging both unnecessary, are equally fine — but say which. Required because these two fire once per project; miss the moment and it's gone.
2. Root cause (rides on the root-cause-debugging pointer): before you state a cause or propose a fix, say whether you loaded the skill ("loaded" or "skipped because ...") and which tier your evidence sits in: reproduced here / docs or types / a comment or someone's account. Required because a stated cause leaves the session and is carried forward as fact by everyone who hears it; the failure is the silent skip, not the skipped load.
3. Correction received (rides on the receiving-feedback pointer): before accepting or rebutting a correction — from the user, a reviewer, or a third party — say which it is: holds / breaks / cannot tell yet, and name the evidence that decides it, not who said it. Required because a wrong rebuttal defends the error for another round and a reflexive acceptance imports one; both are paid for in round trips.
4. Blast radius (rides on the blast-radius pointer): before editing a condition, guard, filter, or allow/deny rule, name who reaches it today, grouped by source; "probably nobody" means you are not ready. Required because the miss surfaces in production, weeks later, for someone else.
</no-cape>
EOF
fi

escape_for_json_v escaped "$NOCAPE_CONTEXT"

# Emit only the field the current platform consumes.
# Cursor: additional_context (snake_case, top-level).
# Claude Code: hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext (nested).
# Copilot CLI / SDK standard: additionalContext (top-level).
# printf (not heredoc) avoids the bash 5.3+ heredoc hang.
if [ -n "${CURSOR_PLUGIN_ROOT:-}" ]; then
  printf '{\n  "additional_context": "%s"\n}\n' "$escaped" | cat
elif [ -n "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT:-}" ] && [ -z "${COPILOT_CLI:-}" ]; then
  printf '{\n  "hookSpecificOutput": {\n    "hookEventName": "SessionStart",\n    "additionalContext": "%s"\n  }\n}\n' "$escaped" | cat
else
  printf '{\n  "additionalContext": "%s"\n}\n' "$escaped" | cat
fi

exit 0
